- example: Sword: 1000 G insurance value, 100 G base premium\n2. should have a base premium of 60 G for an amulet -- example: Amulet: 600 G / 60 G\n3. should have a base premium of 80 G for a staff -- example: Staff: 800 G / 80 G\n4. should have a base premium of 40 G for a potion -- example: Potion: 400 G / 40 G\n5. should have a base premium of 25 G per component (rune or moonstone) -- example: Components insured at 250 G each, 25 G base premium\n6. should apply a special base premium of 60 G for exactly 3 alike components (e.g. 3 runes) -- example: 3 runes → 60 G base premium\n7. should not apply a block discount for 4 runes -- example: 4 runes → 100 G base premium\n8. should charge 175 G base premium for 7 runes -- example: 7 runes → 175 G base premium\n9. should not form a block with different types (2 runes + 1 moonstone) -- example: 2 runes + 1 moonstone → 75 G base premium\n10. should form two separate blocks for 3 runes and 3 moonstones -- example: 3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium\n11. should apply 50% surcharge only to the base premium of a cursed sword in a multi-item policy -- example: cursed sword (100 G) and amulet (60 G) → cursed surcharge adds 50 G\n12. should apply item-specific modifiers to item base premium and policy-wide modifiers to policy base premium -- clarification: item-specific vs policy-wide scopes\n13. should apply loyalty discount for a customer with exactly 2 years -- example: customer with exactly 2 years with MHPCO → loyalty discount applies\n14. should apply high-enchantment surcharge for an item with exactly enchantment level 5 -- example: sword with exactly enchantment 5 → high-enchantment surcharge applies\n15. should apply first insurance surcharge to a long-standing customer's new sword -- example: long-standing customer with brand-new sword: first insurance surcharge still applies\n16. should apply 100 G deductible per damage event for each damaged item -- example: dragon attack damages sword (500 G) and amulet (300 G) → payout = 600 G (100 G deductible per item)\n17. should reimburse 400 G for a regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 G -- example: payout 400 G\n18. should reimburse 100 G for a rune, damage 200 G -- example: payout 100 G (full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible)\n19. should reimburse 400 G for a dragon-material sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 G -- example: both clauses apply; 50 % rule wins → payout 400 G\n20. should reimburse 700 G for a dragon-material sword, enchantment 5, damage 800 G -- example: only dragon-material clause applies → payout 700 G\n21. should reimburse 400 G for a steel sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 G -- example: only high-enchantment clause applies → payout 400 G\n22. should reject claim if damages exceed number of items insured -- clarification: two swords damaged when only one insured → reject claim\n23. should set insurance sum to 2000 G and cap to 4"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"nebius/qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-instruct-2507","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1784950311495,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-a843189b-ca58-45a8-8a28-7cab7ff3fe27","responseModel":"Qwen/Qwen3-235B-A22B-Instruct-2507"}}
